.RE
.PP

.SH "Constructor & Destructor Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::PropertyHandle (\fBModel\fP< dim > & sg, const char * var_name, \fBVARIABLE_TYPE\fP ptype = \fRSCALAR\fP, \fBPLACEMENT\fP place = \fRNODE\fP, uint32_t psize = \fR1U\fP)"
Constructor associates a distributed physical variable with the constructed \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&. If the variable already exists in the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP, the new \fBPropertyHandle\fP will be a handle to it\&. If the variable name is not known to the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP, it will be registered as a new variable and property storage will be allocated accordingly by the MemoryManager\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
There are four constructor arguments\&. The first is a reference to the current \fBModel\fP, the second is the variable name, and the remaining two arguments specify the variable type and placement, respectively\&. These last two arguments have the default values SCALAR, and NODE\&. If the variable type or placement differs from these values, they must be specified\&. If a new variable shall be created the user must specify its type and placement\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The constructor initializes the output variable flag to PLAIN and requests the \fBcsmp::Index\fP for the associated variable from the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP\&. It prompts the creation of a new variable if the property does not already exist\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::PropertyHandle (const \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & op)"
A copy-constructed \fBPropertyHandle\fP is associated exactly with the same variable as its input argument\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::~\fBPropertyHandle\fP ()"
The destructor of a \fBPropertyHandle\fP linked to dynamically created variable, will prompt the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP to delete the variable entry and call the MemoryManager to delete the storage which was allocated for that variable\&. 
.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Acos ()"
Returns the arc cos of the physical variable component value\&. This value must be between -1 and 1\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the physical variable component value is not within a range between -1 and 1, an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Asin ()"
Returns the arc sin of the physical variable component value\&. This value must be between -1 and 1\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the physical variable component value is not within a range between -1 and 1, an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Atan ()"
Returns the arc tan of the physical variable component value\&. This value must be between -1 and 1\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the physical variable component value is not within a range between -1 and 1, an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Cos ()"
Returns the cos of the physical variable component value which must be in radians\&. A radian (rad) is defined as the ratio of the corresponding arc length to the radius of the circle\&. Thus, 1 radian = 57\&.2958o and the angle in rad is (grad * pi) / 180\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
It is tested whether the input variable is in the legitimate range of zero to 2 Pi\&. If not, an error message is returned and no calculation is performed\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Exp ()"
Calculates E raised to the power of each component of the physical variable associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> bool \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::IsWithinRange () const"
Tests whether the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP has a value which complies with the legitimate range defined in the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP object\&.

.PP
If the variable was defined at runtime, it is tested against a default range from -1\&.0e+30 to 1\&.0e+30\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
\fBIsWithinRange()\fP returns the boolean variable 'true' if the variable values lie within the expected range\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
sWithinRange() compares the actual value range of the variable with that specified in the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
If so desired by the user, \fBIsWithinRange()\fP will be applied after each operation on a \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> const \fBcsmp::Index\fP & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Key () const"
Returns the storage specification of the variable which is associated with the \fBcsmp::Operand\fP into a corresponding structure\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Ln ()"
Calculates the natural logarithm (base 2) of each component of the CSP basic variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Since the method cannot take the natural logarithm of 0 or a negative number, variables with such a value are not modified\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Log10 ()"
Calculates the decadic logarithm (base 10) of each component of the CSP basic variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Since the method cannot take the logarithm of 0 or a negative number, variables with such a value are not modified\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator*= (const \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & op)"

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(1-F) key\&.type, key\&.place should be opkey\&.type, key\&.place 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator*= (double val)"
Where the variable is flagged that the output flag, the default value of which is ANY, its value is multiplied by the argument value\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator+= (const \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & op)"
The operators '+=', '-=', '*=', and '/=' perform combined arithmetic and assignment operations involving two PropertyHandles but avoid the generation of temporary variables as would be generated, if the operatios were performed separately\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIop\fP The argument to the right of the combined operators must be a \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The value of the physical variable of the \fBPropertyHandle\fP on the right modifies the \fBPropertyHandle\fP value on the left according to the arithmetic operator used\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The operators are based on the overloaded operators of the CSP basic variables, the \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, and \fBTensorVariable\fP\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To add a floating point value to, subtract from, multiply, or divide by the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP, write expressions like:

.PP
.PP
.nf
my_operand += 5\&.2;
.fi
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Operations involving variables of different type or different placement cannot be performed\&. In this case an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator+= (double val)"
The operators '+=', '-=', '*=', and '/=' perform combined arithmetic and assignment operations but avoid the generation of temporary variables as would be generated, if the operatios were performed separately\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIval\fP The argument to the right of the combined operators must be a floating point value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The floating point value on the right modifies the \fBPropertyHandle\fP value on the left according to the arithmetic operator used\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The operators are based on the overloaded operators of the CSP basic variables, the \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, and \fBTensorVariable\fP\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To add a floating point value to, subtract from, multiply, or divide by the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP, write expressions like:

.PP
.PP
.nf
my_operand += 5\&.2;
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator= (const \fBTensorVariable\fP< dim > & ts)"
Assigns the values of the tensor variable to the distributed property\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyHandle\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::operator= (double val)"
Assigns the value of its argument to the physical variable associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP at where the variable has a flag which is equal to the output flag\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Variables of the types double, \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, or \fBTensorVariable\fP may be assigned\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The assignment returns the value into the \fBPropertyHandle\fP to the left of the equal sign\&. The original values are overwritten irrespective of their original flag\&. 
.br
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The assigment operator relies on the overloaded operators of the basic CSMP variables (scalar, vector, and tensor) to perform the assignments\&.

.PP
In the special case, where an assignment of a vector to a scalar is attempted, the length of the vector will be assigned\&.

.PP
The attempt to assign a tensor to a vector is undefined and will therefore provoke an error\&.

.PP
If a tensor shall be assigned to a scalar, the determinant of the tensor will be assigned\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To write \fBPropertyHandle\fP expressions like:

.PP
.PP
.nf
csp_specific_op = 12\&.5;
.fi
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Undefined assigments, like that of a tensor to a vector will invoke error messages\&. Because these are special operations, if the length of a vector or the determinant of a tensor is assigned to a scalar, a warning will be issued which specifies the type of assignment made\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Out () const"
The standard CSP object interface \fBOut()\fP will print the state of the \fBPropertyHandle\fP to stdout\&. This output comprises the name of the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP, the output flag, and the values of the distributed physical variable\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method \fBOut()\fP uses the \fBModel\fP method OutputVariableToScreen() to print the variable values\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Out (const char * text_file_name) const"
The CSP object interface \fBOut()\fP will print the values of the \fBPropertyHandle\fP associated variable to a textfile\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItext_file_name\fP The name of the textfile is supplied as first argument to \fBOut()\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
\fBOut()\fP relies on the \fBModel\fP method OutputDataAsTextColumns() in the creation of the output textfile\&. 
.br
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To save a \fBPropertyHandle\fP temporary variable to file\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Refer to the documentation of the \fBModel\fP to learn about the footprint of OutputDataAsTextColumns()\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::OutputCondition () const"
\fBOutputCondition()\fP returns the \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP flag which the distributed physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP must have in order to allow modification by the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The flag that the physical variables flag must correspond to if the variable shall be modified\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Since each CSMP scalar, vector, or tensor variable has a single or a set of flags which indicate to the solver whether it may modify or use this variable instance as a constraint, the same rule applies to PropertyHandles\&. Thus, only if the output flag matches the local flag of the distributed variable, it will modify the latter\&. Accordingly, the variable flag may be used to protect certain variable values from modification by PropertyHandles\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::OutputCondition (\fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP c)"
\fBOutputCondition()\fP assigns the \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP flag which the distributed physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP must have in order to allow modification by the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIc\fP The flag which the output flag shall be changed into\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
The flag of the physical variables flag must correspond, else the variable is not modified\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Since each CSMP scalar, vector, or tensor variable has a single or a set of flags which indicate to the solver whether it may modify or use this variable instance as a constraint, the same rule applies to PropertyHandles\&. Thus, only if the output flag matches the local flag of the distributed variable, it will modify the latter\&. Accordingly, the variable flag may be used to protect certain variable values from modification by PropertyHandles\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Pow (double raised_to)"
Raises each component of the CSP basic variable associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP to the power of its first argument\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIraised_to\fP The power to which the variable shall be raised\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Range (double & omin, double & omax) const"
Performs a range check for the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&.

.PP
\fBRange()\fP will return the minimum and the maximum value of the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP into its first and its second argument\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
\fBRange()\fP calls the MinMaxOf() interface of the \fBModel\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Sin ()"
Returns the sin of the physical variable component value which must be in radians\&. A radian (rad) is defined as the ratio of the corresponding arc length to the radius of the circle\&. Thus, 1 radian = 57\&.2958o and the angle in rad is (grad * pi) / 180\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
It is tested whether the input variable is in the legitimate range of zero to 2 Pi\&. If not, an error message is returned and no calculation is performed\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Sqrt ()"
Takes the square root of the values of the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Squared ()"
Multiplies the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP by itself\&. If this variable is vector, or a tensor, the corresponding vector or matrix products will be assigned, respectively\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method relies on the interface of the CSP basic variables \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, and \fBTensorVariable\fP\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Apart from offering an efficient way to raise variable values to the power of 2, \fBSquared()\fP implements vector and matrix multiplication for vector and tensor variables, respectively\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::Tan ()"
Returns the tan of the physical variable component value which must be in radians\&. A radian (rad) is defined as the ratio of the corresponding arc length to the radius of the circle\&. Thus, 1 radian = 57\&.2958o and the angle in rad is (grad * pi) / 180\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
It is tested whether the input variable is in the legitimate range of zero to 2 Pi\&. If not, an error message is returned and no calculation is performed\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyHandle\fP< dim >::ZapNAN (double with)"
Replaces each NAN (not a number) value of the physical variable which is associated with the \fBPropertyHandle\fP with the supplied floating point argument\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwith\fP The floating point number with which NAN values shall be replaced\&. 
